# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-3.0

config MESHTASTIC_DIAG_LOOP_STACK_SIZE
	int "Stack size for the combined thread-summary/supervisor diagnostic loop"
	default 2048
	depends on MESHTASTIC_THREAD_SUMMARY || MESHTASTIC_SUPERVISOR
	help
	  agents-wu94.12.1: mt_thread_summary (was CONFIG_MESHTASTIC_THREAD_SUMMARY_STACK_SIZE,
	  2048 B) and mt_supervisor_thread (was CONFIG_MESHTASTIC_SUPERVISOR_STACK_SIZE,
	  4096 B) merged onto one thread with one stack, replacing both symbols with
	  this one.

	  2026-09-21: deliberately provoking the supervisor's alert() path on real hardware
	  (rzr4, class 6) found a genuine crash-loop, not a tight margin -- confirmed via a
	  full decoded backtrace (Xtensa EXCCAUSE 63, Zephyr's own z_check_stack_sentinel
	  catching a real overflow): thread_analyzer_run()'s callback calling check_thread()
	  for mt_diag_loop's OWN entry, once it crosses CONFIG_MESHTASTIC_SUPERVISOR_STACK_WARN_PCT,
	  fires alert() -> alert_phone() -> meshtastic_phoneapi_on_packet() ->
	  meshtastic_phoneapi_enqueue_fromradio() (672 B alone, measured with -fstack-usage)
	  -> meshtastic_phoneapi_notify_data_ready() -> a BLE work-queue submit -> a kernel
	  reschedule -- roughly 1.6 KB of measured frames across ~13 nested calls, all
	  running on mt_diag_loop's own stack, self-referentially triggered by mt_diag_loop
	  warning about its own stack pressure. Doubling this Kconfig's default to 4096 B did
	  NOT fix it -- identical crash, identical ~41 s timing, both sizes -- because the
	  true worst case was never a sizing problem: nobody had ever measured this specific
	  chain (it was pure dead code until the alert path actually fired), and its depth
	  isn't a small fixed increment over the routine 39-53%/2048 B baseline.

	  FIX (2026-09-21, agents-wu94.12.1): alert()'s actual phoneapi send moved off this
	  thread entirely, onto a dedicated worker (CONFIG_MESHTASTIC_SUPERVISOR_ALERT_STACK_SIZE,
	  Kconfig.supervisor) -- the same "isolate the deep call chain onto its own measured
	  thread" pattern agents-ooma.35 already proved for XEdDSA signing two days earlier
	  in this codebase (main/src/meshtastic_pki.c). mt_diag_loop itself never runs that
	  chain anymore, so its own worst case is back to the routine sweep+dispatch cost --
	  2048 B restores real margin over the 39-53% baseline this Kconfig was originally
	  sized from, which was always accurate; it was the untested alert path riding on
	  the SAME stack that was the actual defect, not this number.
